sections.body:
This lesson's lab has two processors. The receipt processor is new: for each class marked `@Moving`, it writes a `<Name>Receipt` data class with the same properties plus `signedBy`, and a `receipt(signedBy)` function to make one. The receipt carries `@Inventory`, the sticky note that asks for an inventory card. The second processor is lesson 2's card processor, copied into the lab and rewritten with KotlinPoet, which writes `inventoryCard()` for every `@Inventory` class. Used from code, the two outputs look like this:

```java
// Java calling the same generated Kotlin: extension functions are static methods of their file's class
LampReceipt lampReceipt = LampReceiptKt.receipt(new Lamp("desk lamp", 1, null), "Ana");
System.out.println(LampReceiptInventoryCardKt.inventoryCard(lampReceipt));
```

```kotlin
val lampReceipt = Lamp("desk lamp", 1, null).receipt(signedBy = "Ana")
println(lampReceipt.inventoryCard())   // LampReceipt: name=desk lamp, bulbs=1, shade=null, signedBy=Ana
```

The card processor must write a card for a class that the receipt processor writes during the same build. How can it see that class?

The answer is rounds. A round is one pass in which KSP calls `process()` on every processor, one after another, in the order of the services file. Each call gets a new `Resolver`, the object a processor asks for symbols. In round 1, the Resolver offers the source files. When a round is over, KSP reads the files written during it, and the next round offers those, plus the symbols set aside the round before. A new round starts only if the previous one wrote at least one file. javac, which has run Java's annotation processors since Java 6, works in rounds too. KSP is Kotlin's own, faster way to do the same job for Kotlin code.

To watch the rounds, count them. KSP's docs say it "creates a processor instance only once", so a field lives from round to round. The Resolver must not be kept: each round brings a new one. Both lab processors log each round with `logger.warn`, so it shows at Gradle's default log level:

```java
// Closest Java equivalent: javac calls process() once per round, on the same processor object
private int round = 0;

@Override
public boolean process(Set<? extends TypeElement> annotations, RoundEnvironment roundEnv) {
    round++;
    processingEnv.getMessager().printMessage(Diagnostic.Kind.WARNING,
            "[receipts] round " + round + ", processingOver=" + roundEnv.processingOver());
    // ... find, check, read, write
    return false;
}
```

```kotlin
private var round = 0      // KSP creates the processor once, so the field lives across rounds

fun processReceipts(resolver: Resolver): List<KSAnnotated> {
    round++
    // ... find, check, read, write
    logger.warn("[receipts] round $round: wrote $written, set aside ${later.map(::nameOf)}")
    return later
}
```

Here is `./gradlew :ksp:app:kspL06Kotlin` on the lab's playground, with `@Moving` on `Lamp` and `Van`, and `@Inventory` on `Box`:

```text
w: [ksp] [receipts] round 1: wrote [LampReceipt], set aside [Van]
w: [ksp] [cards] round 1: wrote cards for [Box]
w: [ksp] [receipts] round 2: wrote [VanReceipt], set aside []
w: [ksp] [cards] round 2: wrote cards for [LampReceipt]
w: [ksp] [receipts] round 3: wrote [], set aside []
w: [ksp] [cards] round 3: wrote cards for [VanReceipt]
w: [ksp] [receipts] round 4: wrote [], set aside []
w: [ksp] [cards] round 4: wrote cards for []
w: [ksp] [receipts] finish() after 4 rounds
```

Read it as a story. In round 1 the receipts processor wrote `LampReceipt`, and the cards processor, running right after it, still didn't see it: it found only `Box`. In round 2 the new file was offered to everyone, and `LampReceipt` got its card. Each round wrote something, so another followed, until round 4 wrote nothing and KSP ended with `finish()`. Four rounds for three classes.

In the picture, each round is a walk through the house, and the cards written on one walk are new boxes for the next. The picture breaks in one way. Real helpers work side by side, while KSP's take turns, and a card that helper A writes is invisible to helper B until the next walk.

**What a round writes, the next round reads.**

That leaves `Van`, set aside in round 1. Why it had to wait is the next step.

